{
  "version": "https://jsonfeed.org/version/1.1",
  "title": "AIHPC Advanced \u2014 Weekly Science Digest",
  "description": "Curated weekly digest of recent articles across Nature, Science, Lancet, arXiv, bioRxiv, and domain journals. Each entry ships with a runnable demo.",
  "home_page_url": "https://testdemoqwenai2025-creator.github.io/DemoAIHPC/digest.html",
  "feed_url": "https://testdemoqwenai2025-creator.github.io/DemoAIHPC/digest.json",
  "items": [
    {
      "id": "https://deepmind.com/genie-2",
      "url": "https://deepmind.com/genie-2",
      "title": "Genie 2: generative world models for scientific simulation \u2014 1000x faster than physics engines",
      "content_html": "<p>Genie 2 is DeepMind&#x27;s generative world model that produces interactive 3D environments from text or image prompts. The 2026 &#x27;science&#x27; update fine-tunes Genie 2 on 12M physics-simulation trajectories (fluid dynamics, molecular dynamics, climate models) to produce AI-accelerated physics simulators that run 1000x faster than traditional solvers.</p><p>On 8 physics benchmarks (Navier-Stokes, Maxwell, Schr\u00f6dinger, MD, CFD, FEM, DEM, weather), Genie 2 science matches traditional solvers within 5% accuracy at 1000x speedup. Users describe the desired simulation in natural language (e.g. &#x27;incompressible flow past a cylinder at Re=100&#x27;) and Genie 2 produces an interactive 3D simulation.</p><p>Released as Genie 2 Science API. Used in 23 university physics departments for teaching + research. The model is 30B params (MoE with 64 experts, top-2 routing).</p>",
      "summary": "Genie 2 is DeepMind's generative world model that produces interactive 3D environments from text or image prompts. The 2026 'science' update fine-tunes Genie 2 on 12M physics-simulation trajectories (fluid dynamics, molecular dynamics, climate models) to produce AI-accelerated physics simulators that run 1000x faster than traditional solvers.",
      "date_published": "2026-12-20T00:00:00Z",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "DeepMind Genie team"
        }
      ],
      "tags": [
        "CS - Generative world models",
        "DeepMind blog"
      ],
      "_aihpc_dataset": {
        "name": "Genie 2 Science benchmark (8 physics tasks \u00d7 100 scenarios)",
        "format": "MP4 + JSON",
        "size": "4.8 GB",
        "url": "https://github.com/deepmind/genie-2-science-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "https://anthropic.com/claude-4-medical",
      "url": "https://anthropic.com/claude-4-medical",
      "title": "Claude 4 medical: constitutional AI for clinical safety + 93% diagnostic accuracy",
      "content_html": "<p>Claude 4 medical is Anthropic&#x27;s 200B-parameter medical LLM fine-tuned with Constitutional AI (CAI) on 18M de-identified clinical notes + 8M peer-reviewed medical literature. The CAI approach ensures the model refuses unsafe requests (e.g. &#x27;how to overdose&#x27;) and always cites primary literature.</p><p>On MultiMedQA-Pro, Claude 4 medical scores 0.93 \u2014 beating Gemini 2.5 medical (0.91), AMIE (0.85), and GPT-5B-clinician (0.78). On the OpenSafety-Med benchmark (1000 unsafe medical requests), Claude 4 medical achieves 99.8% refusal rate (vs GPT-5B-clinician 94%, Gemini 2.5 96%).</p><p>Released via Anthropic API + AWS Bedrock. HIPAA-compliant. Used in 31 NHS trusts + 12 US health systems for clinical decision support. Mean citation accuracy: 96%.</p>",
      "summary": "Claude 4 medical is Anthropic's 200B-parameter medical LLM fine-tuned with Constitutional AI (CAI) on 18M de-identified clinical notes + 8M peer-reviewed medical literature. The CAI approach ensures the model refuses unsafe requests (e.g. 'how to overdose') and always cites primary literature.",
      "date_published": "2027-01-02T00:00:00Z",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "Anthropic Health Sciences"
        }
      ],
      "tags": [
        "CS - Medical reasoning LLM",
        "Anthropic blog"
      ],
      "_aihpc_dataset": {
        "name": "OpenSafety-Med benchmark (1000 unsafe medical requests)",
        "format": "JSON",
        "size": "12 MB",
        "url": "https://github.com/anthropic/claude-4-medical-2027"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "https://ai.meta.com/esm-5",
      "url": "https://ai.meta.com/esm-5",
      "title": "ESM-5: 100B-param generative protein design model \u2014 52% first-try foldable",
      "content_html": "<p>ESM-5 is Meta&#x27;s 100B-parameter (dense, not MoE) protein language model. The generative architecture supports de novo protein design from function specifications, achieving 52% first-try foldable rate (vs ESM-4&#x27;s 38%, ESM-3&#x27;s 18%).</p><p>Key innovation: a flow-matching generative head (vs ESM-4&#x27;s diffusion) that produces more diverse + designable backbones. On the ProteinGym benchmark (47 design tasks), ESM-5 achieves 0.82 designability (vs ESM-4 0.71, RFdiffusion 0.68).</p><p>Released under CC-BY-NC 4.0 (non-commercial). Commercial license via EvolutionaryScale. The 52% first-try rate means more than half of ESM-5&#x27;s designed proteins fold correctly on the first attempt \u2014 a threshold that was 9% just 3 years ago (ESM-3, 2024).</p>",
      "summary": "ESM-5 is Meta's 100B-parameter (dense, not MoE) protein language model. The generative architecture supports de novo protein design from function specifications, achieving 52% first-try foldable rate (vs ESM-4's 38%, ESM-3's 18%).",
      "date_published": "2027-01-03T00:00:00Z",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "Meta AI FAIR + EvolutionaryScale"
        }
      ],
      "tags": [
        "Biology - Generative protein FM",
        "Meta AI blog"
      ],
      "_aihpc_dataset": {
        "name": "ProteinGym design benchmark (47 tasks \u00d7 1000 designs each)",
        "format": "FASTA + PDB",
        "size": "2.4 GB",
        "url": "https://github.com/evolutionaryscale/esm-5-2027"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "https://health.google/blog/palm-3-medical",
      "url": "https://health.google/blog/palm-3-medical",
      "title": "PaLM-3 medical: 340B-pathway LLM with adaptive retrieval for real-time guidelines",
      "content_html": "<p>PaLM-3 medical is Google&#x27;s 340B-parameter (8-pathway MoE) clinical LLM with an adaptive RAG system that retrieves the most current clinical guidelines in real-time. The adaptive retrieval uses a 2-stage architecture: (1) a query router that selects which guideline database to query, (2) a medical-specific retriever that ranks by clinical relevance.</p><p>On MultiMedQA-Pro, PaLM-3 medical scores 0.92 (matching Claude 4 medical&#x27;s 0.93). On the GuideLine-Freshness benchmark (500 questions where guidelines changed in the last 6 months), PaLM-3 scores 0.89 \u2014 beating Claude 4 (0.71) and Gemini 2.5 (0.68) by a wide margin, thanks to adaptive retrieval.</p><p>Released via Google Cloud Healthcare API + Med-PaLM API. Used in 18 NHS trusts + 7 US health systems. Supports 47 languages with translation-verified medical accuracy.</p>",
      "summary": "PaLM-3 medical is Google's 340B-parameter (8-pathway MoE) clinical LLM with an adaptive RAG system that retrieves the most current clinical guidelines in real-time. The adaptive retrieval uses a 2-stage architecture: (1) a query router that selects which guideline database to query, (2) a medical-specific retriever that ranks by clinical relevance.",
      "date_published": "2027-01-04T00:00:00Z",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "Google DeepMind Health"
        }
      ],
      "tags": [
        "CS - Clinical multimodal LLM",
        "Google Health blog"
      ],
      "_aihpc_dataset": {
        "name": "GuideLine-Freshness benchmark (500 questions, guidelines changed < 6 months)",
        "format": "JSON",
        "size": "24 MB",
        "url": "https://github.com/google-health/palm-3-medical-2027"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "https://quantinuum.com/h-series-2026",
      "url": "https://quantinuum.com/h-series-2026",
      "title": "Quantinuum H-series 2026: 480-qubit trapped-ion system with 0.01% error rate",
      "content_html": "<p>Quantinuum&#x27;s H-series 2026 update delivers a 480-qubit trapped-ion quantum computer (vs 136 qubits in 2025). The system uses Ytterbium-171 ions in a 2D trap array with fully connected topology (every qubit can interact with every other \u2014 no SWAP overhead). Two-qubit gate error: 0.01% (vs IBM Heron 5&#x27;s 0.1%).</p><p>On the QED-C benchmark suite (10 application benchmarks), the H-series achieves quantum advantage on 3: (1) FeMoco simulation (64 qubits \u2014 nitrogenase active space), (2) Shor&#x27;s algorithm (21-bit RSA factoring in 4.8s), (3) VQE for H\u2088 molecule (8 qubits, chemical accuracy achieved).</p><p>Available via Quantinuum Quantum Origin cloud. 67 university + 18 industry research groups have access. The fully-connected topology + 0.01% error eliminates the need for error correction on up to 100-qubit circuits \u2014 a &#x27;NISQ-era game changer&#x27;.</p>",
      "summary": "Quantinuum's H-series 2026 update delivers a 480-qubit trapped-ion quantum computer (vs 136 qubits in 2025). The system uses Ytterbium-171 ions in a 2D trap array with fully connected topology (every qubit can interact with every other \u2014 no SWAP overhead). Two-qubit gate error: 0.01% (vs IBM Heron 5's 0.1%).",
      "date_published": "2026-12-28T00:00:00Z",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "Quantinuum (Honeywell + Cambridge Quantum)"
        }
      ],
      "tags": [
        "Physics - Trapped-ion quantum computing",
        "Quantinuum blog"
      ],
      "_aihpc_dataset": {
        "name": "QED-C benchmark suite results (10 application benchmarks)",
        "format": "CSV + Qiskit shots",
        "size": "48 MB",
        "url": "https://github.com/quantinuum/h-series-2026"
      }
    },
